== Film version ==
In 1965, the serial was adapted by [[Milton Subotsky]] as a film, ''[[Dr. Who and the Daleks]]'', starring [[Peter Cushing]] as [[Dr. Who (Dalek films)|Dr. Who]], [[Roberta Tovey]] as Susan, [[Roy Castle]] as Ian Chesterton and [[Jennie Linden]] as Barbara. The film also received a sequel in 1966, ''[[Daleks' Invasion Earth 2150 A.D.]]'', based on the 1964 serial ''[[The Dalek Invasion of Earth]]''.{{sfn|Ainsworth|2015|p=158}}

== ''The Daleks in Colour'' ==
A 75-minute [[colourised]] version of the serial, titled ''The Daleks in Colour'', was announced on 2 November 2023, to celebrate ''Doctor Who''{{'}}s [[Doctor Who specials (2023)|60th anniversary]].<ref name="Radio Times 60th"/> It features a new score by [[Mark Ayres]], original sound design, and new material written by incumbent showrunner [[Russell T Davies]] and recorded by original Dalek voice actor David Graham.<ref name="Radio Times 60th Date"/><ref name="Radio Times 60th RTD"/> Among the new material written by Davies was "Exterminate!", the Daleks' famous battle cry missing from the original story.<ref name="DWM Yearbook"/> Davies sought to colourise old ''Doctor Who'' serials shortly after returning the role of showrunner in early 2022, considering himself "the [[curator]] of a museum in some sense".<ref name="SMH Colour"/>{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=52}} ''The Daleks in Colour'' was edited by [[Benjamin Cook (journalist)|Benjamin Cook]].<ref name="Screen Rant 60th"/>

[[File:The Daleks in Colour - colour palette.png|thumb|upright=1.2|For the [[Film colorization|colourisation]], each character was allocated a [[Color scheme|colour palette]] for consistency.{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=52}}|alt=A infographic showing a still with an old man and a young girl. Underneath them, a colour palette of the scene is shown.]]

Rich Tipple led the project as lead colourisation artist after ''Doctor Who'' executive producers Joel Collins and [[Phil Collinson]] saw his fan-made work on [[YouTube]].{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=52}} Production began in November 2022 and ran for ten months;{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|pp=52–53}} Tipple was joined by Timothy K. Brown, Scott Burditt, and Kieran Highman. Characters had individual [[Color scheme|colour palettes]], and each team member was allocated a different location for consistency: Tipple oversaw the Dalek city, Brown the TARDIS control room, Burditt the caves, and Highman the jungle.{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=52}} The team sought to honour the original serial while making it appealing to new viewers.{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=53}}<ref name="BBC Colour"/>

Colour photographs from the original filming were consulted but creative license was encouraged; instead of imitating the original sets, the team sought to create a version "if ''Doctor Who'' had [[Star Trek: The Original Series|original ''Star Trek'']] money to produce something in colour, on colour cameras".{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=52}} Discussions were required for some colours, such as the TARDIS console, which was painted green during filming to avoid [[camera flare]] but intended to look white, and Barbara's shirt, which was originally blue but made pink in the colourisation to avoid poor contrast.{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=52}} Some changes included Skaro's red sky becoming green in the evenings.{{sfn|Dunlop|2024|p=53}}

''The Daleks in Colour'' aired on BBC Four on 23 November<ref name="Radio Times 60th"/> to 350,000 overnight<ref name="Twitter Freeman"/><ref name="Twitter Brown"/> and 499,000 consolidated viewers,<ref name="DWM Viewers"/> before becoming available on [[BBC iPlayer]].<ref name="Radio Times 60th"/> It was released on DVD and [[Blu-ray]], alongside the original seven-part serial, on 12 February 2024 in the United Kingdom,<ref name="Colour DVD UK"/> 19 March in the United States,<ref name="Colour DVD US"/> and 4 September in Australia.<ref name="Colour DVD Aus"/> It was also available as a [[SteelBook]] release.<ref name="Colour DVD"/> ''[[Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]''{{'}}s Tristram Fane Saunders complimented the editing, and praised the original serial's production design, cinematography, and score.<ref name="Telegraph Colour Review"/> ''[[Bleeding Cool]]''{{'}}s Adi Tantimedh lauded the faster pacing for heightening performances and characterisation, though found some cuts too abrupt, flashbacks unnecessary, and picture quality unstable.<ref name="Bleeding Cool Colour Review"/> ''[[Comic Book Resources]]''{{'}}s Andrew Gladman considered Ayres score "a driving force" for the story's faster pace.<ref name="CBR Colour Review"/>

